\newlabel{fig:transfer}{{2}{4}{Each row is a rhythm optimised for one fluid, scored in five (speed relative to the plain sinusoid; boxed $=$ its own fluid). The $De$-specific optimum, and the magenta entry $0.914<1$: the low-memory champion is slower than the sinusoid in the high-memory fluid.\relax }{figure.caption.3}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Mechanism. (a) Difference in stored elastic stress between the two rhythms at matched shape, at three Deborah numbers spanning the crossover; the sign structure inverts. Beads: small (magenta) and large (blue). (b) Running polymer displacement through one stroke; the finishing order (markers) reverses across $\mathit  {De}_{c}$.\relax }}{5}{figure.caption.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:mech}{{3}{5}{Mechanism. (a) Difference in stored elastic stress between the two rhythms at matched shape, at three Deborah numbers spanning the crossover; the sign structure inverts. Beads: small (magenta) and large (blue). (b) Running polymer displacement through one stroke; the finishing order (markers) reverses across $\Dec $.\relax }{figure.caption.4}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Reduced-order theory (Fourier algebra, no solver). (a) The pair term is identical for the two rhythms (curves coincide). (b) The triple term is exactly antisymmetric and crosses zero --- the reversal. (c) Within the single-mode family the crossover collapses under $\mathit  {De}_{c}\langle \dot  \theta ^2\rangle $ across swimmer geometry and confinement; this collapse does \emph  {not} extend to other modulation shapes (\S  \ref  {sec:disc}).\relax }}{5}{figure.caption.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:theory}{{4}{5}{Reduced-order theory (Fourier algebra, no solver). (a) The pair term is identical for the two rhythms (curves coincide). (b) The triple term is exactly antisymmetric and crosses zero --- the reversal. (c) Within the single-mode family the crossover collapses under $\Dec \langle \dot \theta ^2\rangle $ across swimmer geometry and confinement; this collapse does \emph {not} extend to other modulation shapes (\S \ref {sec:disc}).\relax }{figure.caption.5}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Reinforcement learning. (a) The strategy an agent learns from reward alone at each fluid; colour is the learned rhythm, which flips from open to closed at the crossover. (b) Learning curves: reward relative to the sinusoid, climbing as the agent learns.\relax }}{6}{figure.caption.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:rl}{{5}{6}{Reinforcement learning. (a) The strategy an agent learns from reward alone at each fluid; colour is the learned rhythm, which flips from open to closed at the crossover. (b) Learning curves: reward relative to the sinusoid, climbing as the agent learns.\relax }{figure.caption.6}{}}
